      {
        "name": "sentinelone",
        "description": "State of the local SentinelOne agent, as reported by `sentinelctl status`. One row when SentinelOne is installed, no rows when it isn't.",
        "platforms": [
          "darwin",
          "windows",
          "linux"
        ],
        "evented": false,
        "examples": "Full SentinelOne status for a host.\n\n```\nSELECT * FROM sentinelone;\n```\n\nReport which hosts have SentinelOne protected and talking to the management console. Hosts without the agent return no rows.\n\n```\nSELECT agent_version, protection, management_connected FROM sentinelone;\n```\n\nUse as the query for a policy that passes only when the agent is protecting the host and connected to the console.\n\n```\nSELECT 1 FROM sentinelone WHERE protection = 'enabled' AND management_connected = 'yes';\n```\n\nFind hosts that haven't checked in with the SentinelOne console in over a day.\n\n```\nSELECT agent_id, management_server, management_last_seen FROM sentinelone\nWHERE management_last_seen != '' AND management_last_seen < (strftime('%s', 'now') - 86400);\n```\n\nFind hosts where a SentinelOne daemon isn't ready.\n\n```\nSELECT agent_id FROM sentinelone\nWHERE service_agent_helper NOT IN ('', 'ready')\n   OR service_guard NOT IN ('', 'ready')\n   OR service_framework NOT IN ('', 'ready');\n```",
        "notes": "This table is not a core osquery table. It is included as part of Fleet's agent ([fleetd](https://fleetdm.com/docs/get-started/anatomy#fleetd)).\n\nValues are the status strings `sentinelctl` reports, passed through unchanged. A column is an empty string when the installed agent doesn't report that field, so an empty value means \"not reported,\" not \"off.\"\n\n`sentinelctl` on Windows reports a smaller, flatter set of fields than on macOS and Linux. Where a Windows field has a macOS equivalent it populates the same column: agent build ID fills `agent_version`, disable state fills `operational_state`, SentinelMonitor fills `es_framework`, and SentinelNetworkMonitor fills `network_monitoring`. The remaining Windows-only fields have their own columns (`tamper_protection`, `agent_run_mode`, `mitigation_policy`), and the columns Windows doesn't report at all (threat protection, daemons, launchd, management) are empty.\n\nBecause `sentinelctl` needs root to report full status, this table depends on osquery running as root (macOS and Linux) or LocalSystem (Windows), which is how fleetd runs it.",
        "columns": [
          {
            "name": "agent_version",
            "description": "Version of the installed SentinelOne agent. On Windows, taken from the monitor build ID.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "name": "agent_id",
            "description": "Unique ID the SentinelOne console uses for this agent.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "install_date",
            "description": "Unix timestamp of when the agent was installed. Empty when the reported date can't be read.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "es_framework",
            "description": "State of the agent's kernel-level event source, `started` or `stopped`. Apple's EndpointSecurity framework on macOS, SentinelMonitor on Windows.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "name": "operational_state",
            "description": "Whether the agent is operating, e.g. `enabled` or `disabled`. On Windows, derived from whether a user disabled the agent.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "name": "remote_profiler",
            "description": "State of the agent's remote profiler.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "network_monitoring",
            "description": "State of the agent's network monitoring, e.g. `started` or `stopped`.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "name": "network_extension",
            "description": "State of the agent's network extension.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "network_extension_content_filter",
            "description": "State of the network extension's content filter.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "ready",
            "description": "Whether the agent is ready, `yes` or `no`.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false
          },
          {
            "name": "protection",
            "description": "Whether the agent's threat protection is on, e.g. `enabled` or `disabled`. Windows reports its anti-tamper state instead, in `tamper_protection`.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "infected",
            "description": "Whether the agent has found an unresolved threat on the host, `yes` or `no`.",
            "type": "text",
            "required": false,
            "platforms": [
              "macOS",
              "Linux"
            ]
          },
          {
            "name": "network_quarantine",
            "description": "Whether the host is network quarantined by SentinelOne, `yes` or `no`.",
            "type": "text",
